-- title:
--   Dual-isogeny series for the transported rigidification and the correspondence identity
-- statement:
--   The statement is set up over a large body of data, which falls into the following groups.
--
--   *Coefficients.* Two primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, a nonzero level $N$ with $r \nmid N$; a characteristic-zero integral domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring, an irreducible element $\pi \in \mathcal O$ such that $\mathcal O$ is $\pi$-adically complete, with $\#(\mathcal O/\pi) = r$ and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (so $\mathcal O$ is absolutely unramified with residue field of cardinality $r$), and a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   *The unramified extension.* A characteristic-zero integral domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra, together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to: $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$; that ideal is maximal; every $x \in O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ whose value at $x$ lies in $(\pi)$; every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $(\pi)$; and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^r \pmod{(\pi)}$ for all $x$. Thus $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ plays the role of the completed maximal unramified extension of $\mathcal O$ with its Frobenius.
--
--   *Quaternionic data.* Rationals $a, b$ with the hypothesis `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b r rbar`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of the ring of integers of $\mathbb Q$, all nonzero elements of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ are units exactly when $v$ contains $r$ or $\bar r$. A maximal order $\Lambda \subseteq \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ (an order maximal among orders) with $\Lambda \supseteq \mathbb Z$, and a map $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to \mathbb{Z}_{r^2} \times \mathbb{Z}_{r^2}$, where $\mathbb{Z}_{r^2} =$ `Zp2 r` is the Witt ring of $\mathbb F_{r^2}$, satisfying `IsOrderCoord`: additivity, $\mathrm{coord}(1) = (1,0)$, the twisted multiplication rule $\mathrm{coord}(mm') = (m_1m'_1 + r\, m_2 \varphi(m'_2),\ m_1 m'_2 + m_2 \varphi(m'_1))$ with $\varphi$ the Witt-vector Frobenius, injectivity, density of the image modulo every power of $r$, and the trace condition $m_1 + \varphi(m_1) = n$ whenever $m + \bar m = n$.
--
--   *Data in characteristic $r$.* A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ for $(\Lambda, N)$ over the residue ring $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ of dimension $2$ over that ring (a two-dimensional formal group law with an action of $\mathbb{Z}_{r^2}$ and a uniformiser endomorphism $\varpi$ with $\varpi \circ \varpi = [r]$ and $\varpi \circ [\alpha] = [\varphi(\alpha)] \circ \varpi$) of height $4$, formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of dimension $2$ along the unit section of $A_0$, and the hypothesis that $\theta_0$ exhibits $X_0$ as the formal module of $A_0$ via $\mathrm{coord}$ (`IsFormalModuleVia`: $\theta_0$ are formal coordinates for the relative group law of $A_0$ with group law $X_0.F$, and the $\Lambda$-action on points corresponds to the series $[\mathrm{coord}(m)_1] + [\mathrm{coord}(m)_2]\circ\varpi$).
--
--   *Drinfeld-side data.* A ring homomorphism $\iota : \mathbb{Z}_{r^2} \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}$; a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ which is special for the reduction of $\iota$ (the zero and one graded pieces of its Lie algebra are complementary invertible modules) and of height $4$; a moduli package $M$ over $(r, O^{\mathrm{nr}})$ which is a Zariski sheaf; and a family $\eta$ assigning to each ring $B$ with a homomorphism $\psi$ from $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ in which $r$ is nilpotent a map from $\Phi$-rigidified objects over $B$ to $M(B,\psi)$. The hypothesis `hη` has three clauses: over noetherian bases, $\eta$ identifies two admissible rigidified objects precisely when they are isomorphic; $\eta$ commutes with base change along ring maps over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ applied to admissible objects; and every element of $M(B,\psi)$ is, after passing to the members of a finite family of away-localisations whose generators generate the unit ideal, in the image of $\eta$ on an admissible object. Further, an injective ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of $\{\Phi.\mathrm{actEnd}(\alpha)\} \cup \{\Phi.\mathrm{varpiEnd}\}$ into $M_2(K_0)$, together with an integer $m$ such that $r^m \cdot A$ lies in the image of $E_0$ for every $A \in M_2(\mathcal O)$ and $r^m \cdot E_0(e)$ is integral for every $e$.
--
--   *Comparison data.* A ring homomorphism $\kappa : O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi) \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ compatible with the two quotient maps; a natural number $n_0$ and a pair of bivariate power series $\beta_0$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ which is an isogeny $\Phi \to X_0 \otimes_\kappa (O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r))$ of height $4n_0$.
--
--   *Level data and base.* A full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$ with $r \nmid n$; a noetherian commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$, an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$, and the hypothesis that the image of $\pi$ in $B$ is nilpotent.
--
--   *Two curves and an isomorphism.* Fake elliptic curves $E, E'$ for $(\Lambda, N)$ over $B$, an isomorphism $i : E.A \cong E'.A$ with $i_{\mathrm{hom}}$ followed by $E'.f$ equal to $E.f$, and `IsoVia`: $i_{\mathrm{hom}}$ respects the relative group laws, commutes with the $\Lambda$-actions, and matches the level subschemes in both directions.
--
--   *Rigidifications and the correspondence.* Rigidifications $\varrho$ of $E$ and $\varrho'$ of $E'$ relative to $(r, \pi, A_0, \psi)$; each consists of a curve $E_b$ over $B/(\pi)$ pulled back from $E$, a curve $A_b$ over $B/(\pi)$ pulled back from $A_0$ along the residue map, an integer $d$, and a pair of mutually dual isogenies $\varphi : E_b \to A_b$, $\varphi'$ with composites $[r^d]$, preserving the level. Morphisms $ib : \varrho.E_b.A \to \varrho'.E_b.A$ with $ib$ followed by $\varrho'.g_b$ equal to $\varrho.g_b$ followed by $i_{\mathrm{hom}}$, and $ib$ followed by $\varrho'.E_b.f$ equal to $\varrho.E_b.f$; a morphism $u_A : \varrho'.A_b.A \to \varrho.A_b.A$ which exhibits $\varrho'.A_b$ as the pullback of $\varrho.A_b$ along the identity of $B/(\pi)$ and satisfies $u_A$ followed by $\varrho.g_A$ equal to $\varrho'.g_A$; and natural numbers $i_1, j_1$ with the correspondence identity
--   $$ib \cdot \varrho'.\varphi \cdot u_A \cdot \varrho.A_b.\mathrm{act}(r^{i_1}) \;=\; \varrho.\varphi \cdot \varrho.A_b.\mathrm{act}(r^{j_1})$$
--   in diagrammatic order, the actions being those of the integers $r^{i_1}, r^{j_1} \in \Lambda$.
--
--   *Matching level structures and a section.* Full level-$n$ structures $P_n$ on $E$ and $P'_n$ on $E'$ with $P'_n$ obtained from $P_n$ by pushing along $i_{\mathrm{hom}}$; a morphism $Q : \operatorname{Spec}(B/(\pi)) \to \varrho.A_b.A$ which is a section of $\varrho.A_b.f$ and whose composite with $\varrho.g_A$ is the level point $P_0$ of $A_0$ pulled back along the residue map $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi) \to B/(\pi)$ induced by $\psi$.
--
--   *Formal module of $E$ and the rigidified object.* A formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X$ over $B$ and formal coordinates $\theta$ of dimension $2$ for $E$ with $E.\mathrm{IsFormalModuleVia}\ \mathrm{coord}\ X\ \theta$; an integer $j \le 1$ and a $\Phi$-rigidified object $t$ over $B$ with $t.X = X$, admissible for $\iota$ and the $(-j)$-th Frobenius twist $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-j}$ (so $t.X$ is special and of height $4$, and $t.\rho$ is an isogeny of height $4\,t.n$ from the base change of $\Phi$ to the reduction of $t.X$); and the level compatibility that the reduction modulo $(\pi)$ of the point $[r^{t.n}]P_n$ equals $Q$ followed by $\varrho.\varphi'$ and $\varrho.g_b$.
--
--   *The given series $\sigma$.* A ring homomorphism $\kappa_B : B/(\pi) \to B/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps, and compatible with $\kappa$ in the sense that $\kappa_B \circ \mathrm{residueLeg}(\pi,\psi) = \mathrm{residueMap}(\psi) \circ \kappa$; a pair of bivariate power series $\sigma$ over $B/(\pi)$ representing the dual isogeny of $\varrho$ on all nilpotent points, namely: for every ring $B''$ which is simultaneously an algebra over $B/(\pi)$, over $B$ and over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$, such that the structure map from $B$ factors through the quotient $B \to B/(\pi)$ and the structure map from $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$ factors through $\mathrm{residueLeg}(\pi,\psi)$, for every ideal $J$ of $B''$ and $m$ with $J^{m+1} = 0$, every $s : \{0,1\} \to J$, and every $P_A : \operatorname{Spec} B'' \to \varrho.A_b.A$ lying over the structure morphism and satisfying $P_A \cdot \varrho.g_A = \theta_0(B'')(s)$, one has $P_A \cdot \varrho.\varphi' \cdot \varrho.g_b = \theta(B'')\big(\mathrm{nilEval}_m(\sigma_i)(s)\big)$. Finally, the factorisation $t.\rho = (\kappa_B)_*\sigma \circ (\mathrm{residueMap}(\psi))_*\beta_0 \circ (X_i \mapsto X_i^{r^j})$ of the rigidification series of $t$.
--
--   The conclusion asserts the existence of a pair of bivariate power series $\sigma'$ over $B/(\pi)$ with three properties.
--
--   First, $\sigma'$ is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules from $X_0$ base-changed along $\mathrm{residueLeg}(\pi,\psi)$ to $X$ base-changed along $B \to B/(\pi)$: it is a homomorphism of the underlying formal group laws, it commutes with the $\mathbb{Z}_{r^2}$-actions, and it commutes with the uniformiser endomorphisms.
--
--   Secondly, $\sigma'$ represents the dual isogeny of $\varrho'$ in the coordinates $\theta_0$ on $\varrho'.A_b$ and $\theta$ transported through $i$: for every ring $B''$ as above (algebra over $B/(\pi)$, over $B$ and over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$, with the same two factorisation conditions on the structure maps), every ideal $J$ and $m$ with $J^{m+1} = 0$, every $s$ with values in $J$, and every $P_A : \operatorname{Spec} B'' \to \varrho'.A_b.A$ lying over the structure morphism with $P_A \cdot \varrho'.g_A = \theta_0(B'')(s)$, one has
--   $$P_A \cdot \varrho'.\varphi' \cdot \varrho'.g_b \;=\; \mathrm{mapPt}(i_{\mathrm{hom}})\Big(\theta(B'')\big(\mathrm{nilEval}_m(\sigma'_i)(s)\big)\Big).$$
--
--   Thirdly, writing $\bar X$ for $X$ base-changed to $B/(\pi)$, the correspondence identity of series
--   $$[\,r^{\,\varrho.d + j_1}\,]_{\bar X} \circ \sigma' \;=\; [\,r^{\,\varrho'.d + i_1}\,]_{\bar X} \circ \sigma,$$
--   where $[\,r^k\,]_{\bar X}$ denotes the action of $r^k \in \mathbb{Z}_{r^2}$ on $\bar X$ and $\circ$ is substitution of series.
--
--   This is a transport step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison between fake elliptic curves with rigidification and Drinfeld's moduli of special formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules: given a series representing the dual isogeny of one rigidification, it produces the corresponding series for a second rigidification obtained across an isomorphism of the underlying curves, together with the identity of series forced by the correspondence relation between the two isogenies. It is used in the proof of [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.Rigidification.isNormLevelTransport_of_isoVia_of_corr_of_isFormalModuleVia`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.Rigidification.isNormLevelTransport_of_isoVia_of_corr_of_isFormalModuleVia).
